 ever the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — R
 EQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 13)\nBy the end of this event\, you should
  be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Read basic schematics\, block dia
 grams\, wiring diagrams\, pinouts\, connector tables\, and signal-flow dra
 wings.\n• Trace power and signals from source to destination and relate 
 schematic symbols to physical hardware.\n• Use datasheets to find pin fu
 nctions\, ratings\, timing\, operating conditions\, and reference circuits
 .\n• Apply a systematic troubleshooting loop: verify symptom\, gather ev
 idence\, divide/isolate\, form hypothesis\, test\, repair\, verify.\n• U
 se diagram/signal-path information to choose measurement points and isolat
 e a fault.\n• Interpret expected values from a schematic before probing.
 \n• Use signal tracing\, substitution/known-good comparison\, continuity
 \, voltage\, and waveform checks appropriately.\n• Distinguish root caus
 e from symptom and document a defensible troubleshooting conclusion.\n\nCO
