 ncise defect/root-causenote.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watching alone never counts.
  Use learn ->retrieve -> calculate/code -> build/measure -> debug -> docum
 ent. Starteach non-exam study block with ~10 minutes of spaced recall. Nev
 er use amissed session as justification for an unhealthy make-upmarathon.\
 n\nTODAY:\nBuild/measure/debug the practical objective.Photograph or captu
 re evidence\, document expected vs actual\, andcommit the lab note/code. C
 ompletion requires demonstration\, notwatching.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP 
 THESE LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your s
 canned/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\
 n• ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nO
 FFICIAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.18 pp.159–164
  + selected Ch.19 pp.165–175 review + Appendix C pp.217–218.\nQUIZ / R
 EVIEW: Ch.18 quiz p.164 + Unit 8 review p.181.\nWHY: This is the book's st
 rongest troubleshooting/diagram/test-point block and directly supports eng
 ineering-lab work.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa 
 textbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ETA errata control when
 ever the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — R
 EQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 13)\nBy the end of this event\, you should
  be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Read basic schematics\, block dia
 grams\, wiring diagrams\, pinouts\, connector tables\, and signal-flow dra
 wings.\n• Trace power and signals from source to destination and relate 
 schematic symbols to physical hardware.\n• Use datasheets to find pin fu
 nctions\, ratings\, timing\, operating conditions\, and reference circuits
 .\n• Apply a systematic troubleshooting loop: verify symptom\, gather ev
 idence\, divide/isolate\, form hypothesis\, test\, repair\, verify.\n• U
 se diagram/signal-path information to choose measurement points and isolat
 e a fault.\n• Interpret expected values from a schematic before probing.
 \n• Use signal tracing\, substitution/known-good comparison\, continuity
 \, voltage\, and waveform checks appropriately.\n• Distinguish root caus
 e from symptom and document a defensible troubleshooting conclusion.\n\nCO
 VERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and tasks above are intended to cover
  every item in this outcome list. If you still cannot explain or demonstra
 te one of these items after the session\, do not count that item as comple
 te. Write the concept on a short catch-up list and use the linked official
  or supplemental resource before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE
 \nThis practical is the cumulative proof point for the week. The week is G
 reen only when the knowledge can be used on real hardware/code/measurement
 s without following a step-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFi
 nish the assigned work\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you ca
 nnot explain or demonstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short
  catch-up list and repair it during the next available review block. No sp
 readsheet logging is required.
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
