 HESE WHEN YOU NEED THEM\n• Week module: https://numberclaw2.github.io/alfr
 ed-university/week.html?week=15\n• Search this topic: https://numberclaw2.
 github.io/alfred-university/search.html?q=C%20Programming%20II%20%2B%20Emb
 edded%20C%20%2B%20State%20Machines\n• Engineering Library: https://nberc
 law2.github.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER CONNECTION: C + MC
 U bring-up + serial buses + debug instrumentation.\nRESOURCE PRIORITY: CET
 a Core → Career Core → Deep Dive. Complete the required work first\; u
 se Deep Dive for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 15 FOCUS: C Programming II
  + Embedded C + StateMachines\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 15.5–15.7\; 13.12reinforc
 ement\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: Pointers\, arrays/strings\,structs/enums\,
  .c/.h modules\, bitwise masks\, const/volatile\, FSMs\,blocking vs nonblo
 cking design.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:\n- Beej’sGuide to C: https://bee
 j.us/guide/bgc/\n- STM32 MCU Developer Zone:https://www.st.com/content/st_
 com/en/stm32-mcu-developer-zone.html\n\nMASTERY GATE: Write modular C usin
 g pointers/structs/enums/bit operationsand explain a finite-state-machine 
 design without copying atutorial.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watching alone never co
 unts. Use learn ->retrieve -> calculate/code -> build/measure -> debug -> 
 document. Starteach non-exam study block with ~10 minutes of spaced recall
 . Never use amissed session as justification for an unhealthy make-upmarat
 hon.\n\nTODAY:\nStudy only the resources tied to this week’scompetencies
 . Create a one-page summary and 5–10 recall prompts. First10 min: spaced
  retrieval from older material.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP THESE LINKS:\n
 • Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your scanned/Google 
 Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n• ETA 6th-e
 d. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY 
 GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.17 pp.153–156.\nQUIZ / REVI
 EW: Ch.17 quiz p.156 + Unit 7 review pp.157–158.\nWHY: Book supplies pro
 gramming/control concepts\; Beej/C exercises provide the real code-writing
  skill needed for the transition.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is th
 e primary CETa textbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ETA erra
 ta control whenever the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAF
 TER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 15)\nBy the end of this eve
 nt\, you should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Use pointers\, add
 resses\, dereferencing\, arrays/pointers\, structs\, and function interfac
 es safely at a practical level.\n• Use bitwise AND/OR/XOR\, shifts\, mas
 ks\, and set/clear/test operations for hardware-oriented code.\n• Explai
 n const\, volatile\, macros\, headers/modules\, and compiler warnings in e
 mbedded C.\n• Distinguish blocking from nonblocking behavior.\n\nCOVERAG
 E AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and tasks above are intended to cover ever
 y item in this outcome list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate on
 e of these items after the session\, do not count that item as complete. W
 rite the concept on a short catch-up list and use the linked official or s
 upplemental resource before closing the gap.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFi
 nish the assigned work\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you ca
 nnot explain or demonstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short
  catch-up list and repair it during the next available review block. No sp
 readsheet logging is required.
